Actually, after playing Fallout 3 for some time now I must say I like=20
it's "repair" system where you use one item to improve on another.

For unihorns (first I thought this was a typo but it's a very useful=20
word :), let's say a unicorn horn initially has 3 charges BUT weighs a=20
lot (a necessary change for this to work). So while you might consider=20
carrying around like two of these max because of the weight you would=20
be given the option to "merge" unicorns. Let's assume you grind the=20
other one to a powder and dip the first one it in... whatever.
Merging would add 1 or 2 (random) charges to the unihorn. But, you=20
aren't as obstructed by weight with just one horn. Even with the large=20
amount of unicorns you might not get more than 10, maybe 15 charges=20
into a unihorn, which is plenty, but still finite (a certain rider or=20
Demo might thus make you suffer). Possibly the merging could be 0-2=20
charges with lesser chance of adding the higher the current charge.

Now, if we would take this further we might do it for armours and=20
weapons as well. Maybe scrolls should be used only to enchance stuff=20
once (i.e. an item cannot be improved more than once and the=20
enchanchement would simply be fooproof+1).
So to make your weapon +3 you would need a similar weapon which you=20
could merge with the original to make it +1 per merge. Could take some=20
time to make +5 weapons :)

And then, if we make it really, really horrible, weapons would=20
downgrade upon usage. So if you are using you +5 silver saber a lot=20
eventually it would become -5 (after which it becomes zorkmids :).=20

Some nice side effects of this would be that you are forced to use=20
different weapons throughout the game as few (good) weapons have an=20
unlimited supply. And better still, artifact weapons would be saved for=20
emergency situations. Using Magicbane for 20 levels would virtually=20
make it a -3 weapon and unique weapons cannot be merged... ouch.

I think a lot of people would hate a chance like this. But well=20
designed it would not make the game abhorrently difficult. And just the=20
idea of being forced to switch weapons more often would make ascensions=20
different, compared to now at least.


